cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use RECALL or cite the Machine Legibility Dimensions framework, please use the metadata below."
title: "RECALL: A COBOL-Inspired Publishing Language for the Web, with Formal Provenance, Semantic Diff, and Machine Legibility Dimensions"
abstract: >
  RECALL is a declarative publishing language with COBOL-inspired syntax that compiles
  to self-contained HTML. It was designed from the foundation for AI compositor use —
  where the primary reader of source is an AI system rather than a human programmer.
  Every compiled artifact embeds its own .rcl source in a structured HTML comment,
  making the artifact self-describing and self-validating. Version 1.1.0 adds the
  AUDIT DIVISION — a formal language construct that records human and AI authorship
  in structured, compiler-validated entries (CREATED-BY constrained to: Human, AI
  compositor, or AI agent). Version 1.1.0 also ships recall diff (semantic AST diff
  between .rcl sources), recall stats (pipeline telemetry), and a full Language
  Server Protocol implementation (recall-lsp). Version 1.2.0 adds recall serve (hot-
  reload dev server) and recall validate (artifact self-validation — extracts embedded
  source, recompiles, and verifies the live artifact matches its own source).
  This repository also contains the Machine Legibility Dimensions (MLD) framework
  (v0.1), a proposed evaluation framework for notations where the primary reader
  is an AI system. MLD covers properties that human-reader frameworks do not address:
  tokenisation alignment, ambiguity surface, state surface, schema availability,
  error signal fidelity, intent density, round-trip fidelity, constraint completeness,
  and decomposability.
